{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"DECISION: land 1717 as-is, then an immediate STACKED follow-up change on this ticket for the\noperation-linked fallback. The ticket stays open until both land.\n\nWhy not a new patchset on 1717: the two mechanisms are genuinely separable — 1717 is the\namortized CI path, the fallback is the everywhere-floor — and they have different blast\nradii and different tests. Folding them together would produce one change that touches a\nworkflow, the sweep selection, the close path and a new detached worker, reviewed as a lump.\n1717 ps2 is also mid-CI right now; amending discards that run for no reviewer benefit. Stacked\nkeeps both units reviewable, which is what CONTRIBUTING asks for.\n\nDESIGN (to be reviewed through the plan gate before I write it):\n\nTrigger, on the close path where `_compact_on_close` used to sit — but AFTER the store lock is\nreleased and after the best-effort push, so nothing the session waits on holds the lock. Two\nO(1) checks, either of which fires:\n  - the ticket just written satisfies the two-arm selection (one listdir + parse of that\n    ticket's own events — independent of store size); or\n  - a last-sweep stamp file is older than a configured interval (one stat). This second check\n    is what makes the floor real: without it, a store whose closed tickets happen not to need\n    folding would never fold the ones that do.\n\nExecution: spawn a DETACHED worker that runs the full `compact-all` sweep, reusing\n`enrich_drain._spawn_detached_drain`'s shape verbatim — the PYTHONPATH bootstrap for the bare\npython child, `_detach_kwargs()` (start_new_session/close_fds on POSIX, DETACHED_PROCESS on\nWindows), stdio to DEVNULL and a log file. The session returns immediately; the fold happens\nwith the same lock profile as a manual `rebar compact`, which is the accepted baseline, and\nnever across an LLM call.\n\nStorm control: a stamped advisory lock beside the drain lock in `.rebar/`, using the SAME v2\nownership stamp and the SAME shared decision table `lock_owner.stamped_file_is_stale` that\nchange 1704 just fixed for the drain lock — so pid-recycle qualification, refusal-without-proof\nand the wall-clock ceiling are inherited, not re-invented. One compactor at a time; a caller\nthat finds the lock held simply does not spawn.\n\nWindows is a v1 no-op, mirroring the drain, because the store lock's fcntl import would crash a\ndetached child there.\n\nA tri-state config knob mirroring `overlap_drain` (async detaches / always runs inline for\ntests and CI / off), so the suite can exercise the fold synchronously and an operator can\ndisable it.\n\nThis does NOT reintroduce the P0: the P0 was that an operation an agent WAITS ON held the lock\nfor 13m53s. Here the waiting operation holds nothing, and the worker's hold is bounded by the\nsame fold the scheduled sweep performs.\n\nNote this changes ticket material, and the completion-verification close gate checks\nplan-review validity for a story, so the description gets this design and a fresh passing\nreview before anything lands."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786613623044504001,"uuid":"8ed6a85b-cd02-48d9-bc8e-ef141e3a1950"}